# JarvOS continuity — denylist. Wins over dotfiles.allow, always.
#
# Patterns are bash `[[ path == pattern ]]` globs matched against the
# HOME-RELATIVE path. Note that `*` crosses `/` here, so `*.env` matches
# `.config/fish/conf.d/keys.env` and `.claude/projects/*` matches everything
# below that directory.
#
# The rule of the file: err towards refusing. A skipped file costs the user one
# manual copy; a leaked one costs a rotation. Denials are counted and reported,
# never silent.

# ── secrets: excluded by construction, never staged, never read ──────────
.secrets/*
*/.secrets/*
.env
.env.*
*.env
*/.env
*/.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.pfx
*.p12
*.jks
*.keystore
*.kdbx
*.gpg
*.asc
*.ovpn
*.mobileconfig
id_rsa*
*/id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*/id_ed25519*
id_ecdsa*
*/id_ecdsa*
id_dsa*
*/id_dsa*
.netrc
*/.netrc
.git-credentials
*/.git-credentials
.npmrc
.pypirc
.ssh/*
.gnupg/*
.aws/*
.azure/*
.config/gcloud/*
.docker/config.json
.password-store/*
# Name-shaped denial, restricted to data files. Deliberately NOT bare `*token*`:
# that also swallows source the user wants (`hooks/redact-secret-output.py`,
# `skills/vault-working/`). Source files are instead policed by the content gate
# in `secret-patterns.txt`, which refuses any file carrying a secret-shaped
# string whatever it is called.
*credentials.json
*.credentials.json
*token*.json
*token*.txt
*token*.conf
*token*.ini
*token*.yaml
*token*.yml
*secret*.json
*secret*.txt
*secret*.conf
*secret*.ini
*secret*.yaml
*secret*.yml
*password*.json
*password*.txt
*password*.conf
*apikey*.json
*api_key*.json
secrets-catalog.md
*/secrets-catalog.md

